rify coordinates: G4(X), H5(X), I6(X), J7(X).\n    -   This forms a contiguous line of 4 stones.\n    -   The ends of this line are F3 (6,3) and K8 (11,8).\n    -   Both ends appear to be empty and within the board boundaries.\n    -   This is an **Open 4**, which is typically a guaranteed win for Black because even if White blocks one end, Black can play the other to complete 5-in-a-row.\n2.  **White\'s Status:** White has a cluster of stones around columns F-J and rows 6-9, specifically a vertical group at I7-I8-I9.\n    -   Playing I10 would create a 4-in-a-row (I7-I10), but it would be blocked at I6 by Black, making it a "Sleeping 4". This is not an immediate win and does not force Black to respond if Black has a faster win (which he does).\n    -   White does not have any move that creates an immediate 5-in-a-row or an Open 4 to counter-attack instantly.\n3.  **Strategy:** Since White is facing an immediate loss, the only valid option to maximize chances (or prolong the game) is to block one of the winning spots for Black.\n    -   Option A: Block at **F3**.\n    -   Option B: Block at **K8**.\n\n**Reason:**\nWhite must block the diagonal Open 4 immediately. Between the two blocking options (F3 and K8), **K8** is preferable. K8 is adjacent to White\'s existing stone at I8 (and close to I7/I9/J6), which keeps White\'s stones more connected for potential future plays if the game continues. F3 is more isolated from White\'s main forces. Although Black can still win by playing F3, K8 is the strongest defensive move available.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 8}'}
